    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-007-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The interwar global economy reconnected trade, capital and domestic politics in unstable ways. The systemic crisis after 1929 intensified unemployment, financial stress and policy pressure, while fascist movements converted social dislocation into highly concentrated political mobilization."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Economic shock, social response and political choice must remain distinct. Crisis can widen the space for particular forms of action without mechanically determining a single political outcome; institutions, coalitions and historical conditions still matter."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article distinguishes historical fact, historical reconstruction and structural interpretation in the manuscript. Later outcomes are not treated as inevitable at the time, and motives or causal claims not present in the manuscript are not added."
        }
      ]
    }
